---
// The walkthrough hub, and the teaching surface of the cluster.
//
// THE THREE LAYERS LIVE HERE, NOT ON NINE PAGES. The device pages are procedures
// in the same sense /checklist is a procedure: they render an ordered list of
// steps and they teach nothing a lesson does not already own. Writing "how a
// hardware wallet setup actually works" nine times would be nine copies of one
// argument, and this project has already paid for four copies of a table of
// contents and two copies of a rung pitch. So the mechanism, the alternatives —
// including the ones we advise against — and the recommendation are stated once,
// on the door, and the nine pages inherit them.
//
// THE `signing` DIAGRAM IS REUSED RATHER THAN REDRAWN. It already says the exact
// thing this page opens on: the key stays inside, and what comes out is a
// signature. A second drawing of that would be a second rendering of one subject.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import RuleRef from '../../components/RuleRef.astro';
import { navWhere } from '../../data/nav.js';
import {
  walkthroughGroups, walkthroughDevices, walkthroughVerified, walkthroughFor, drawnNotCaptured,
  screenPanels,
} from '../../data/walkthrough.js';
import { numberWord } from '../../data/numbers.js';

const wallets = navWhere('/wallets');
const finder = navWhere('/find-your-setup');
---
<Base
  title="What setting up a hardware wallet actually looks like · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description={`Drawn, step-by-step walkthroughs of the ${numberWord(screenPanels.length)} screens every cold-storage hardware wallet shows you during setup — the PIN, your words, the check, and the address — with a page for each device we rate for cold storage. No affiliate links.`}
  verified={walkthroughVerified}
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Before you take it out of the box</p>
    <h1>What setting one up <span class="accent">actually looks like</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Almost everything written about hardware wallets stops at which one to buy. The part that
      decides whether your Bitcoin survives happens twenty minutes later, on a screen the size of a
      postage stamp, in a room on your own. These pages are that twenty minutes, drawn — one for
      each device we rate for cold storage.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">These are drawings, and we would rather say so</span>
      <p style="margin:0">{drawnNotCaptured}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="how-it-works">Why the device has a screen at all</h2>
    <p>
      A hardware wallet is not a safe with your Bitcoin inside it. Your coins are on the Bitcoin
      network and always were; what the device holds is the one secret that can move them, and its
      entire job is to hold that secret somewhere your computer cannot reach — and then to
      <em>show you</em> what it is about to do, on a display your computer does not control.
    </p>
    <Diagram
      name="signing"
      caption="The device never hands over the key. Your computer asks it to approve something; the device shows you what that something is, you press the button, and only a signature comes back."
    />
    <p>
      That second half is the one people miss, and it is what makes the setup steps below matter
      rather than merely exist. Your computer can be lying to you — about the address you are
      sending to, about the wallet you are looking at, about which words you just typed. The little
      screen is the one surface in the whole arrangement that an attacker on your laptop cannot
      repaint. <strong>Every screen in these walkthroughs is a moment where the device is telling
      you something your computer is not allowed to tell you, and your only job is to actually read
      it.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The keys themselves come from randomness the device generates when you first set it up, and
      the words it shows you are that randomness written in a form a human can copy down. Which is
      why the words <em>are</em> the wallet: the device is a convenient way to use them, and it is
      replaceable. The words are not.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-else">The other ways people do this — including the two we would talk you out of</h2>
    <p>
      You have almost certainly already thought of one of these, so here they are with what each one
      actually costs you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Follow the maker’s own guide instead.</strong> Genuinely a good idea, and every page
      here links to it. Their guide is the authority on which menu item is called what this month,
      and it is kept current in a way nothing on this site could be. What it will not do is tell you
      which of its own defaults to refuse, because it is not in the business of arguing with itself
      — and on more than one device here the default is not what we would choose.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Follow a video.</strong> Fine for seeing the physical device handled. The trouble with
      video is that it is undated in practice — a walkthrough filmed two firmware versions ago looks
      exactly as authoritative as one filmed last week, and the comments never say which. If you
      watch one, check its date against the maker’s guide before you copy anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Have somebody set it up for you.</strong> This is the one we would talk you out of, and
      not because of trust. Whoever does the setup sees the words. Even where that person is
      completely trustworthy, you end up holding coins whose recovery you have never once performed —
      and the first time you do it will be under pressure, on the day something has already gone
      wrong. Far more Bitcoin has been lost this way than was ever stolen —
      <RuleRef key="simplest-setup" label="the rule about the simplest setup that covers you" /> is
      built on that, and none of it is about villains.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Buy a device that arrives ready to go.</strong> This is the other one, and here it
      really is about trust: a device that shows you a seed phrase it has already generated is a
      device someone else has the words to.
      <RuleRef key="buy-direct" label="Buy it new and direct" /> exists because that scam is cheap,
      common, and completely invisible until the coins leave.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Set it up, and skip the restore test.</strong> Most people do this, including people
      who have held Bitcoin for years. It is the reason this walkthrough has a step almost no
      published guide has: the backup you have not restored from is a piece of paper you
      <em>believe in</em>. <RuleRef key="test-backup" /> is the whole difference between the two.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-do">What we would do</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Set aside an hour, alone, with the door shut and the phone in another room.</strong>
      Not because the setup takes an hour — it does not — but because the two failure modes here are
      being rushed and being watched, and neither is fixable afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Do the steps in the order on your device’s page, and do not skip ahead to funding
      it.</strong> That order is not ours to shuffle: it is the same order your checklist runs in,
      and the build refuses to publish a walkthrough that reorders it. The single most expensive
      mistake available to you in that hour is moving real money in before the restore has been
      proven, because everything looks completely fine either way.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Keep the maker’s guide open beside ours.</strong> Theirs has the exact wording and the
      exact menu; ours has what to refuse and why. They answer different questions and you want both
      open at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And if the device does something these pages did not lead you to expect, stop and find
      out why before you continue.</strong> That is not a counsel of paranoia. It is the one moment
      in the life of this setup when stopping costs you nothing at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="devices">Pick your device</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Grouped by how you actually drive the thing, because that is what changes what you see —
      two devices from one maker can be further apart here than two devices from different ones.
    </p>
    {walkthroughGroups.map((g) => (
      <div class="grp">
        <p class="grp-h"><span class="mono">{g.ui.controls}</span></p>
        <ul class="cards">
          {g.devices.map((d) => (
            <li>
              <a class="card" href={walkthroughFor(d.slug)}>
                {d.image && <img src={d.image} alt="" width="64" height="64" loading="lazy" />}
                <span class="c-body">
                  <span class="c-name">{d.name}</span>
                  <span class="c-sub">{d.vendor}</span>
                  {/* The roster is the surface where a reader picks a device to
                      read about, so an unflagged tile here is the site quietly
                      offering nine equal choices while two of its other pages
                      say otherwise. The tile is not removed: the walkthrough
                      still exists for owners, and hiding it would answer the
                      endorsement objection by abandoning the people it serves. */}
                  {d.notRecommended && <span class="c-nr">{d.notRecommended.flag}</span>}
                </span>
                <span class="arw">→</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="why-these">Why these devices and not every device</h2>
    <p>
      This cluster covers the devices we rate <strong>built for cold storage</strong>, and it stops
      there on purpose. A step-by-step walkthrough is an endorsement wearing a diagram’s clothes: it
      makes a device easier to follow onto, and doing that for hardware our own published standard
      says we would not put a decade of savings on would be arguing with ourselves in a format
      nobody reads as an argument. The two devices we rate for spending and the one that does not
      clear our bar are covered in full on <a href="/wallets">{wallets.label}</a>, with the reasoning
      attached — they are simply not walked through here.
    </p>
    <p>
      The list above is not typed anywhere. It is the cold tier, read from the same file that
      decides the ratings, so a device cannot quietly appear here without clearing the bar or
      disappear from here while still clearing it.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Not sure which device yet? <a href="/find-your-setup">{finder.label}</a> settles what kind of
      setup you need first, which is the question that decides how many devices you are buying at
      all. There are {walkthroughDevices.length === 1 ? 'one walkthrough' : `${walkthroughDevices.length} walkthroughs`} here
      and you only need the one.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose :global(ul) { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .prose :global(li) { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  .prose :global(li::marker) { color: var(--accent); }

  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .grp { margin: 0 0 1.6rem; }
  .grp-h { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; color: var(--faint); font-size: 0.74rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase; }

  .cards { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.7rem; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }

  .card { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.9rem; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--surface); padding: 0.85rem 1.1rem;
    text-decoration: none; color: inherit; transition: border-color 0.15s ease, transform 0.15s ease; }
  .card:hover { border-color: var(--accent); transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .card img { width: 48px; height: 48px; object-fit: contain; flex: none; }
  .c-body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1 1 auto; }
  .c-name { font-weight: 700; color: var(--text); }
  .c-sub { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--faint); }
  .c-nr { display: block; margin-top: .2rem; font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: .05em;
          text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--warn); }
  .arw { color: var(--accent); transition: margin-left 0.15s ease; }
  .card:hover .arw { margin-left: 0.25rem; }
</style>
